## 3. Design Decisions & Tradeoffs Summary

| Decision | Why | Tradeoff |
|----------|-----|----------|
| **SHA-256 content hashing** | Cryptographic hash — collision resistance for regulatory data integrity | More expensive than xxHash or MD5, but the data volumes are small (hundreds of records per source) |
| **`session.flush()` inside engine, `commit()` in caller** | Engine accumulates changes; caller controls transaction boundary | Requires discipline — forgetting to commit loses work; forgetting to rollback on error leaves dirty state |
| **Module-level scraper registry** | Simple, explicit, discoverable | Not dynamically extensible — adding a new agency requires code change + redeploy |
| **`agencies` as Postgres ARRAY** | Simple schema, no join table | Doesn't scale to millions of subscriptions with complex agency filters |
| **`dispatched` as boolean** | Simple delivery tracking | No retry logic, no partial delivery tracking per subscriber |
| **Unified diff as text in `diff_summary`** | Human-readable, directly usable | Not queryable — can't search for "all changes affecting CASRN X" |
| **APScheduler in-process** | Zero operational overhead, co-located with app | If the process dies, scheduling dies with it. No distributed coordination |
| **`keepends=True` in difflib** | Preserves exact line endings in diffs | Slightly larger diff output |
| **FastAPI `dependency_overrides` for testing** | Elegant, built-in FastAPI feature | Test code must understand the override mechanism |
| **CORS wide-open** | Development convenience | Production hardening needed |

